{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"A plain BF16 fast path is already about 4× faster for large workloads, but rounding the two activation gradients loses too many `grad_x` elements for 1–16 tokens. I found a compact fix: represent each activation gradient as a BF16 high part plus a BF16 residual only for the small-token cases. That preserves the FP32 result while retaining matrix-core GEMMs. I’m now fusing activation/rounding and weight packing into two Triton kernels."}}
